    "## 2. The question: decision, action, cost of a wrong call\n",
    "\n",
    "**Search question:** Which pages in a client's content inventory should be reviewed first for refresh, expansion, or protection — based on observable search and engagement signals measured over the prior 90 days?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Unit of analysis:** One content item (one page), scored at a point in time. One row = one page.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Output:** A ranked review queue — pages ordered by priority score, each with a suggested action (refresh, expand, protect, monitor, prune) and reason codes a human reviewer can read and trust.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Who acts on it:** A content strategist or SEO lead. They open the queue at the start of the week and work top-to-bottom. The model decides the order; they decide what to do about each page.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cost of a wrong recommendation:**\n",
    "- *False positive* (flagging a healthy page): wastes a writer's time on a page that did not need work.\n",
    "- *False negative* (missing a declining high-traffic page): leaves a page deteriorating until it falls off page one — lost impressions and clicks that are hard to recover.\n",
    "- The false negative is more expensive, so recall on high-impression pages matters more than overall accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why data or ML can help:** No human can review 500,000 pages. A fixed rule-based score can rank, but it treats all signals with fixed weights and cannot learn which combinations of signals actually predict future decline. A model can find non-obvious patterns — pages that look acceptable on any single metric but are quietly failing across three signals at once."
   ]
  },
