    "## 4. Careful words: what I can and can't claim\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this work CAN say:**\n",
    "- *Observed:* Pages with these signal combinations were more likely to be declining in this dataset, over this time window.\n",
    "- *Directional:* Higher scores in this queue suggest higher review priority, based on patterns in historical data.\n",
    "- *Decision-support:* This ranking helps a human reviewer spend limited time on the most urgent candidates first.\n",
    "- *Comparative:* A learned ranking measurably outperforms the fixed baseline rule on Precision@50, on the starter slice, using client-holdout validation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this work CANNOT say:**\n",
    "- It cannot claim that refreshing a page *caused* a traffic recovery. Causation requires an experiment or a valid causal design — this data alone does not support that claim.\n",
    "- It cannot claim to predict Google's algorithm or explain how rankings are determined by search engines.\n",
    "- It cannot claim AI citations, AI search rankings, or AI visibility — the `ai_sessions` field measures click-throughs from AI tools, not AI platform rankings.\n",
    "- It cannot claim results on the full warehouse generalize from the starter slice — the full analysis must be validated separately.\n",
    "- It cannot promise that a high-scoring page will recover after review. The output is a *prioritized candidate list*, not a guarantee.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How I will phrase claims throughout:** \"We observed,\" \"this suggests,\" \"in this dataset,\" \"based on these signals\" — never \"this proves\" or \"this predicts Google.\""
   ]
